  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Scoping nature-based solutions Sidoardjo</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Sidoarjo Regency in East Java is an important producer of traditionally farmed tiger shrimp and milkfish for global markets. In recent years, aquaculture productivity has declined due to decreasing salinity in key drainage creeks, notably the Kepetingan River. This change is linked to sedimentation-driven shallowing, reduced tidal exchange, and loss of mangrove ecosystems, resulting in progressively fresher water conditions unsuitable for brackish-water species.At the request of the Government of Indonesia and East Java Province authorities, Ecoshape carried out a scoping mission in December 2022. The mission assessed causes of declining aquaculture productivity and explored sustainable, system-based solutions following Building with Nature principles.The study concluded that conventional dredging provides only short-term relief. Instead, sustainable improvement requires nature-based interventions such as restoring intertidal wetlands, rehabilitating mangroves at the river mouth, and integrating flood retention measures to enhance tidal exchange and sediment management. These measures can deliver multiple benefits, including improved aquaculture yields, flood risk reduction, biodiversity enhancement, and carbon sequestration.Additional recommendations include capacity building through farmer field schools, low-cost pond improvements, supportive local legislation, and targeted monitoring of salinity, water levels, subsidence, and coastal dynamics. A first-order cost&#x2013;benefit analysis was identified as a critical next step to enable informed decision-making and potential implementation. The local authorities increased monitoring efforts, following the recommendations. Additionally, from 2024 to 2025 Fish Farmer Field Schools were established, of which progress and results are reported under initiative PVW524ID05.</narrative>
  </description>
